          Croatia pioneered the European digital nomad movement in 2021 and remains the continent's most tax-advantaged program. Under Article 48 of the revised Croatian Aliens Act:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Statutory Income:</strong> Calculated at 2.5 times the average net monthly salary of the previous year, setting the 2026 baseline at approximately <strong>€2,539 per month</strong> (or a deposited lump sum of €30,468 in liquid savings for a 12-month stay).</li>
          <li><strong>Total Tax Immunity:</strong> Under Article 9 of the Croatian Income Tax Act, non-resident digital nomads receive a <strong>100% complete exemption from Croatian personal income tax</strong> on remuneration received from non-Croatian entities.</li>
          <li><strong>Schengen Access:</strong> Since Croatia's formal accession into the Schengen borderless zone, nomad permit holders enjoy unrestricted mobility across Western and Central Europe.</li>
          <li><strong>The Drawback:</strong> Croatia limits nomad permits to 1 year and does not permit consecutive back-to-back renewals. Applicants must wait 6 months outside Croatia before reapplying, or transition to a standard residency basis.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Spain -->
      <section>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
